            <ArticleTitle>Health information in material safety data sheets for a chemical which causes asthma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>89-93</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the quality of health information on material safety data sheets (MSDS) for a workplace chemical that is well known to cause or exacerbate asthma (toluene diisocyanate, TDI).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">We reviewed a random sample of 61 MSDSs for TDI products produced by 30 manufacturers.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Two physicians independently abstracted data from each MSDS onto a standardized audit form. One manufacturer provided no language about any respiratory effects of TDI exposure. Asthma was listed as a potential health effect by only 15 of the 30 manufacturers (50%). Listing asthma in the MSDS was associated with higher toluene diisocyanate concentrations in the product (P &lt;.042). Allergic or sensitizing respiratory reactions were listed by 21 manufacturers (70%).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Many MSDSs for toluene diisocyanate do not communicate clearly that exposure can cause or exacerbate asthma. This suggests that physicians should not rely on the MSDS for information about health effects of this chemical.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
